# Catchline visual standard

## Authority and scope

Close translation of the existing goal renders, confirmed by Mo on 2026-10-15.
The fishing view sets the bar; harbour, menu and subsequent features inherit it.
Art/STYLE.md remains authoritative for model geometry, palette and materials.

## World

An inviting miniature working harbour. Broad bevels, honey timber, ivory rails,
navy paint, warm sunlight, cool ambient fill and small busy sailors. No global
outlines, noisy texture detail, heavy bloom, depth of field or glittering ocean.
Frame the boat generously, leaving quiet water for interface and interactions.

## Tokens

| Role | Value |
|---|---|
| Ink / navy | #173F57 |
| Cream surface | #F4EFE3 |
| Primary action | #E36A35 |
| Sea / secondary action | #168F9F |
| Water highlight | #64C7C4 |
| Bite / attention | #E6AD38 |
| Success | #3E9A5B, accompanied by words or an icon |
| Error | #C8443A, accompanied by recovery copy |
| Typography | supplied Fredoka Medium; navy on cream, ivory on navy |

## Composition and components

- Fishing: compact cream status panel upper left; coins/pause/return upper
  right; portrait crew strips lower left; action bubbles attached to the task.
  The centre belongs to the boat. Strike panel occupies lower-centre water.
- Use supplied sliced artwork, icons and fish/crew portraits consistently.
  Icons get real space; do not pad button strings with spaces to fake alignment.
- Stable readable type hierarchy, generous internal padding, short copy and
  grouped resources. Avoid rows of tiny debug statistics.
- Catch reward: restrained portrait, species, weight, value and record badge.
  Never cover the Carry action or open a blocking reward modal.
- Menu: supplied illustrated harbour, real logo and left-aligned cream menu
  with one clear primary action. Settings and pause share the component family.
- Harbour shops use the same typography, illustrated headers, item icons,
  accessible controls and explicit owned/unavailable states.

## Motion

Calm layered sea colour and broad moving light patterns. Narrow broken hull
contact foam; wakes only while travelling. No dense white bars crossing the sea.
Feedback enters briefly and settles; animations must not delay actions.

Harbour departure first slides sideways clear of the quay, then turns onto the
selected fishing ground's bearing. Return reverses the journey, aligns parallel
and slides into berth. Turn about the hull centre; the camera keeps the whole
manoeuvre readable. Water highlights stay restrained enough to show shallow
seabed and underwater kelp/fish; dense white sheen is rejected.

Sound is quiet tactile coastal foley, confirmed by Mo on 2026-10-16. The captain's
cast, splash, bite and reel are audible; crew automation stays quiet. Keep the
sea bed subdued, avoid melodic arcade rewards, and expose a sound control.

## Acceptance

Compare actual native captures to the references at desktop and landscape-phone
sizes. Verify waiting/bite/reel/catch/carry/full/return states, menus, affordability
and save behaviour. Preserve deliberate changes in shared components; fix those
components rather than independently reskinning each feature later. Native Unity
render/input checks replace browser/Lighthouse checks for this project.

## Revised world requirement â€” POLISH-003

Author the complete camera-visible fishing-location patch as a cohesive miniature
coastal scene: connected shoreline and cliff forms, grass/vegetation, deliberate
working-coast detail and submerged shelf/seabed. Derive dimensions from gameplay
camera coverage, with framing margin. Mo's 10x20 m example expresses bounded
scope, not literal dimensions. Generic scattered pale rocks are rejected.

Use detailed Blender-authored static environment geometry with live Unity water,
boat, shadows and interactions. Water must support animated normals/waves,
shore/hull contact, shallow/deep variation and per-location colour controls.
Evaluate Plunder's adapted Boat Attack package before inventing another minimal
water shader. A beauty render is a composition target; runtime parity must be
checked independently. The UI still requires a faithful rebuild against goal
renders, especially References/day03-small-boat.png; POLISH-002 is not accepted.

### Water direction â€” Mo, 16 October 2026
Clean stylized blue 3D water with broad rounded wave shapes visible from the
fishing camera. Use the current PTS clean preset as the reference. Reduce fine
surface noise; keep white glints sparse and foam/wash disabled. Codex 1 owns the
water adaptation; Codex 2 owns the new skiff work and its separate design brief.
# Catchline â€” art style and modelling contract

Status: concept and first-prop handoff for `art/style-pass`. The PNG sheets are modelling references. Actual Blender 
meshes and FBX exports now accompany them: the [six Crab Coast props](Models/Props/REPORT.md) and [26 Gull Harbour 
assets](Models/GullHarbour/REPORT.md). Unity was not opened for either pass. The gameplay prototype and its 
scenes/scripts are outside this branch's changes.

## Visual authority

Mo's [Day 3 small-boat](Concepts/References/day03-small-boat.png) and [Day 28 
tuna-boat](Concepts/References/day28-tuna-boat.png) mockups are the visual targets. Preserve their inviting miniature 
working-boat character: navy hulls, warm timber, ivory rails, orange safety equipment, small busy sailors, sea teal 
and soft sunlight. Aim for their shape language and material warmth as closely as possible. The PNGs in `Concepts/` 
adapt that look into isolated orthographic references for modelling; they are not screenshots of a finished Unity 
build.

Use chunky silhouettes, broad bevels, a few deliberate planar facets and clean colour blocks. Low-poly does not mean 
every surface is triangulated visibly. Round heads, gloves and boots can use smooth normals; hull corners, tools and 
machinery retain selected flat planes. Keep most detail in silhouettes and large parts rather than texture noise, tiny 
rivets, scratches or complicated piping.

**Outline decision: no global outlines.** Separate shapes using value, colour, bevel highlights and restrained contact 
shading. Do not add black inverted-hull or screen-space outlines to every object. A selection ring or local 
interaction highlight is a separate gameplay affordance.

## Palette

These are proposed canonical **sRGB base colours**, chosen to match the mockups' visual families. They are not pixel 
samples from lit renders. Exact values here override generated colour-chip text and image sampling. Lighting will 
change their apparent colour.

| Use | Hex | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Deck wood | `#C58B50` | Warm honey timber; broad planks, sparse seams |
| Wood highlight | `#DFB77A` | Restrained lighter plank variation |
| Wood recess | `#8C5A36` | Seams and sheltered underside only |
| Hull navy | `#173F57` | Hull, frames, captain garments |
| Hull lit blue | `#2A6685` | Optional secondary painted panel |
| Rail white / ivory | `#F3F0DC` | Rails, cabin, crate rims; avoid pure white |
| Safety orange-red | `#E36A35` | Equipment, beanie, buoy, lifering, trim |
| Sea teal | `#168F9F` | Sea, sorter bib and trays |
| Shallow-water highlight | `#64C7C4` | Broad moving accents, not glitter |
| Yellow oilskins | `#E6AD38` | Deckhand and engineer helmet |
| Steel | `#8FA6AC` | Sorting surface, simple metal details |
| Rubber / deepest shadow | `#28343B` | Boots, roller, rope recesses |
| Rope | `#D1AF76` | Warm rope; `M_Rope` |
| Band ivory | `#F3DEA1` | Pale yellow crab safety bands; `M_BandIvory` |
| Kelp green | `#477A56` | Kelp and pollock back; `M_KelpGreen` |
| Fish silver | `#BAC9C6` | Fish flank; `M_FishSilver` |
| Skin base | `#B9784B` | Initial shared reference; later variants reuse body |
| Sheet / UI cream | `#F4EFE3` | Reference background and future panel family |

Reserve orange for useful accents rather than painting every station orange. Role readability also comes from hat 
silhouette. Status indicators need icons/shape as well as colour; hat colours alone do not communicate station state.

## Materials and light

- Timber: matte, wide planks, subtle broad value variation, little grain. Planks follow the boat's fore-aft axis. Do 
not model every seam as a gap.
- Painted hull and equipment: clean opaque painted surfaces with small bevels. The navy hull should still read blue in 
shadow. Keep metallic response low on paint.
- Steel worktops: muted satin rather than mirrored chrome. Rubber is dark matte. Glass uses dark blue 
opaque/low-complexity glazing for early models; avoid needing cabin interiors.
- Nets and pot mesh: sparse coarse openings, thick readable outer frames. Treat tiny knots as texture or omit them; do 
not create thousands of strands for a concept-matching first asset.
- Water: teal base with restrained broad highlights, gentle motion and a narrow foam/contact treatment around the 
hull. Avoid physically simulated waves or dense reflections as prerequisites for this style.

Lighting target: soft warm sunlight from the upper-left of the gameplay frame, cooler ambient fill, short soft 
shadows, lightly darkened contacts. Maintain readable equipment in shadow. Avoid crushed blacks, strong bloom, depth 
of field, film grain, sharp specular glitter and excessive ambient occlusion. Begin with restrained neutral colour 
grading; saturated palette colours do the work.

**Moving-boat caveat:** a boat that changes layout or moves through world space must not rely on a world-fixed scene 
lightmap for its changing shadows. Achieve the baked-light *look* with controlled materials, optional asset-local 
baked AO/vertex shading, ambient fill and a small real-time lighting setup. Evaluate actual scene baking only for 
genuinely fixed geometry. Moving crew need a compatible dynamic lighting path. Do not stack strong baked AO with 
strong screen-space AO.

URP implementation is a later integration step, not part of these concepts. [Unity 6.3 URP Baked Lit 
reference](https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/Manual/urp/baked-lit-shader.html) and [Unity light 
probes](https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/Manual/LightProbes.html) describe available lighting mechanisms; 
the aesthetic recommendations above are this project's art direction, not a claim that lightmaps can follow arbitrary 
changing layouts.

## Camera and reference conventions

- Game: landscape, fixed orthographic 3/4 camera. Starting art-review angle: about 45 degrees down from horizontal, 
yaw about 35 degrees off the stern axis; tune later for readable jobs and clear walkways.
- Model sheets: true orthographic front/right/back/top, plus an orthographic 3/4 where helpful. A 3/4 view is still 
parallel-projected, never a perspective hero shot.
- Rendered hull top plans and starboard side elevations: bow to the right, stern to the left. In those top plans port 
is up and starboard down. The exact grid supplement deliberately uses bow-up for coordinate readability and labels its 
orientation. Hull plan and elevation must agree about cabin, motor and rails.
- Station front: the operator-facing edge. For the hauler, the plan labels its sea edge on the right and worker edge 
below; mount that sea edge against the rail. Rotation on deck is allowed; dimensions rotate with it.
- Neutral background and soft contact shadow are presentation only; do not bake either into albedo.

Generated multiview sheets can contain small view-to-view differences or misleading ruler/grid marks. **Written 
dimensions, the exact grid supplement and a single coherent model take precedence.** Treat views as shape/colour 
references and reconcile them in Blender; they are not CAD or guaranteed multiview reconstruction inputs. For 
image-to-3D, isolate one subject/view rather than feeding an entire labelled sheet as a single object. Inspect 
resulting topology, scale and hidden surfaces manually.

## World scale and axes

- One deck tile = **1 m Ã— 1 m**. Model in metres, apply transforms before export, use positive unit scale.
- Unity convention: `+Y` up, `+Z` toward bow, `+X` toward starboard. Blender modelling is Z-up; verify export 
conversion so the imported Unity result follows this convention.
- Hull pivot: centre of its rectangular build deck at deck height, `(0, 0, 0)` in local Unity coordinates. Deck 
walking plane is `Y = 0`; hull extends below it.
- Station pivot: footprint centre at deck contact, `Y = 0`. Front working edge faces local `-Z`. Define 
working/interaction points separately from visual meshes.
- Crew root: floor between boots, facing local `+Z`. Hand attachment points are on the shared rig. Crate pivot: centre 
of underside. Crab pivot: ground centre. Buoy pivot: centre of base. Pot pivot: centre of underside. Prop roots use 
their horizontal bounding-box centre with minimum height at zero; variant parts share the same root origin.
- Footprint borders, dimension arrows, labels and scale figures are guides, never mesh geometry.

## Crew: one shared body

Body height is **0.95 m sole to bare scalp**. Hats add approximately 0.06â€“0.10 m above the scalp (module origins 
overlap the head); do not stretch different roles to fit their hats. Proposed shoulder width 0.34 m, head width about 
0.30 m, head height about 0.33 m. Short thick legs, rounded mitten gloves with a thumb, chunky rubber boots, a small 
nose and very simple eyes. Keep fingers, ears and facial features economical. Initial proportions are art targets, not 
anatomy measurements.

All five jobs share body geometry, proportions, skeleton, hand positions and animation set. Hats are removable 
modules; role clothing primarily uses recoloured material regions on the shared garment silhouette. Initial sheets use 
the same skin/hair to make this relationship obvious. Later skin/hair variants do not require a new body or job rig.

| Role | Hat silhouette | Main clothing | Readable cue |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Captain | White peaked cap, navy band | Navy with ivory trim | Wide white cap |
| Deckhand | Orange rolled beanie | Yellow oilskin bib, navy sleeves | Orange + yellow |
| Sorter | Navy baseball cap | Teal bib, navy sleeves | Forward cap peak + teal |
| Bander | Short-brim orange-red cap | Orange-red bib, navy sleeves, ivory gloves | Red-orange workwear |
| Engineer | Yellow hard hat | Muted steel-grey bib, navy sleeves | Helmet dome + brim |

The six reference actions are **walk, carry crate, work at table, idle, cheer and panic**. Use one animation library 
for all roles. For carry, two hands support a 0.45 Ã— 0.32 Ã— 0.25 m crate near waist height; use an attachment 
constraint/pose rather than letting the crate intersect the belly. For table work, the 0.55 m worktop is deliberately 
low enough for a 0.95 m sailor. Cheer and panic must remain distinct at gameplay zoom. Pose sheet drawings are 
key-pose intent, not animation timing or a final rig.

## Hulls

Hull 1's 3 Ã— 2 tile deck and hull 2's 5 Ã— 3 tile deck are confirmed by Mo. Their outer hull dimensions remain 
proposed art envelopes; hull 3's deck and centred wheelhouse footprints are authoritative. Keep hull topology, rail 
thickness, timber treatment and navy/ivory/orange material family consistent across tiers.

| Hull | Overall hull length Ã— beam | Rectangular build deck, fore-aft Ã— across | Fixed content |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 â€” Rowboat | 4.2 Ã— 2.4 m | 3 Ã— 2 tiles | Removable rowing bench, separate oars; no motor/cabin |
| 2 â€” Outboard skiff | 6.5 Ã— 3.5 m, excluding motor | 5 Ã— 3 tiles | One-tile forward console, outboard outside 
stern |
| 3 â€” Day boat | 12.0 Ã— 5.8 m | **10 Ã— 5 tiles** | **3 across Ã— 2 fore-aft tile wheelhouse, centred at bow**, low 
white rails |

Day boat: deck rectangle includes the wheelhouse. It has 50 tiles total, of which 6 are cabin, leaving 44 before 
equipment and aisle allocations. Do not interpret the requirement as a 10 Ã— 5 aft work area plus another cabin area. 
The tapered bow and hull margins are outside the build rectangle.

Exact day-boat deck in local Unity coordinates: `X = [-2.5, +2.5]`, `Z = [-5, +5]`, `Y = 0`. Tile columns `c = 0..4` 
run port to starboard; rows `r = 0..9` run stern to bow. Tile centre is `(c - 2.0, 0, r - 4.5)`. The wheelhouse is 
**centred**, occupying **columns 1, 2 and 3, rows 8 and 9**: **3 m across Ã— 2 m fore-aft**, `X = [-1.5, +1.5]`, `Z = 
[+3, +5]`. One clear tile column remains on each side. Its centre is `(0, 0, 4)` on the deck plane.

Proposed day-boat outer hull extends to `X = Â±2.9`, `Z = [-5.5, +6.5]`. Keep the full build rectangle inside the 
hull; start the bow taper ahead of `Z = +5`. Wheelhouse body height: 1.65 m above deck, with roof, fittings and small 
mast above. Rail height: about 0.60 m. These chibi-scale proportions prioritise a visible deck and matching the 
references over real vessel certification.

Keep cabins, rail sections, motor, oars, hatch covers and fittings modular. Empty hull references intentionally omit 
stations so equipment arrangement stays a gameplay choice. Avoid decorative props blocking walkways or hiding job 
animations.

## Location order

**Location 1 is Gull Harbour**, beginning with manual rod fishing on the 3 Ã— 2 m rowboat deck, then progressing 
towards nets. Harbour Mouth supplies herring/mackerel, Kelpbank pollock/sea bass, and Rocky Point the rare conger eel. 
Cooler storage, bait and voyage provisions support the active gameplay loop. **Crab Coast is location 2.** The older 
crab sheets remain valid asset references, but do not describe the starting location. This follows the live 
`proto/deck-sim` checkoutâ€™s `docs/GAMEPLAY.md`, read before this modelling pass; that working document was not yet 
committed when read.

